{
  "technical_and_design_decisions": [
    "Use Gradle (already in use by Spring XD) with the gradle-rpm-plugin or custom tasks to produce RPM packages.",
    "Use module-level Javadoc comments on @Module annotated classes to deliver both high-level (U2) and detailed (U4) module descriptions, ensuring they surface in the generated Javadoc HTML.",
    "Reuse the built-in Kafka performance testing utilities (kafka-producer-perf-test.sh and kafka-consumer-perf-test.sh) for U5 rather than writing custom benchmark code.",
    "Use the official RabbitMQ perf-test tool (rabbitmq-perf-test) for U1 to produce comparable metrics.",
    "Run benchmarks on EC2 instances identified during the U7 research phase; results are captured as JSON/CSV and aggregated into a Markdown report.",
    "Store RPMs in the spring.io libs-release-local Maven repository layout at the path specified in U3, using the existing Spring release infrastructure (Gradle Artifactory plugin or equivalent).",
    "Add the troubleshooting documentation for U6 as a new section in the existing spring-xd wiki Deployment/Troubleshooting page rather than introducing a new system.",
    "All work is performed by a single developer; no team coordination overhead is included in the estimates.",
    "A workday is defined as 8 productive hours."
  ],
  "assumptions": [
    "The Spring XD build system (Gradle) and module structure are already in place; no foundational refactoring is required.",
    "AWS EC2 access and budget for spinning up test instances is already approved and available; U7 is research and identification, not provisioning itself.",
    "A reasonable number of XD modules (estimated ~20-40 source modules) need Javadoc, and most descriptions can be derived from existing code and existing wiki content.",
    "RabbitMQ and Kafka brokers will be installed/configured on the EC2 instances identified in U7/U8; the developer has working knowledge of both.",
    "The spring.io repository credentials and release permissions are already available to the developer.",
    "Benchmark runs are limited to a single configuration sweep per bus (no full matrix testing); additional sweeps would be follow-up work.",
    "The U1 Rabbit benchmark and the U5 Kafka benchmark use different infrastructure (Rabbit cluster vs. Kafka cluster) and are treated as independent activities.",
    "No production-level hardening, security review, or ongoing maintenance is included in the scope.",
    "Documentation deliverables are Markdown/wiki pages that the developer can author directly."
  ],
  "activities": [
    {
      "activity_id": "A1",
      "activity_name": "Project setup and planning",
      "activity_description": "Review the Spring XD codebase structure, confirm module list, set up local dev environment, and outline the work plan for all user stories.",
      "most_likely_effort": 1
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A2",
      "activity_name": "Module high-level Javadoc descriptions (U2)",
      "activity_description": "Add concise high-level Javadoc to each @Module-annotated class describing the module's overall purpose. Regenerate Javadoc to verify output.",
      "most_likely_effort": 2
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A3",
      "activity_name": "Module detailed Javadoc descriptions (U4)",
      "activity_description": "Add detailed Javadoc on each module describing its purpose, capabilities, options, and usage examples. Includes code review to validate accuracy of descriptions.",
      "most_likely_effort": 3
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A4",
      "activity_name": "RPM build configuration (U3)",
      "activity_description": "Configure the Gradle build to produce Spring XD RPMs (single-node and multi-node variants), including spec file metadata, dependencies, and pre/post install hooks.",
      "most_likely_effort": 2
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A5",
      "activity_name": "RPM publishing to spring.io repository (U3)",
      "activity_description": "Configure the release pipeline to publish the produced RPMs to the spring.io libs-release-local repository path, perform a dry run, and execute a real 1.1.0.RELEASE publish.",
      "most_likely_effort": 2
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A6",
      "activity_name": "Troubleshooting documentation for message header changes (U6)",
      "activity_description": "Investigate changes to message headers between 1.0 and 1.1 releases that may cause serialization errors when reusing 1.0 batch jobs, and document them in the wiki troubleshooting section.",
      "most_likely_effort": 2
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A7",
      "activity_name": "Kafka configuration research (U8)",
      "activity_description": "Research and document the Kafka broker and producer/consumer configurations (num.partitions, replication, batch.size, linger.ms, fetch.min.bytes, etc.) appropriate for the planned performance tests.",
      "most_likely_effort": 2
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A8",
      "activity_name": "EC2 infrastructure research for Kafka (U7)",
      "activity_description": "Identify suitable EC2 instance types, storage (EBS/instance-store), networking, and cluster topology to support the Kafka performance tests; produce a recommendation document with sizing and cost notes.",
      "most_likely_effort": 2
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A9",
      "activity_name": "Kafka benchmarking environment setup (U5)",
      "activity_description": "Provision EC2 instances per the U7/U8 design, install Kafka, and prepare the kafka-producer-perf-test.sh and kafka-consumer-perf-test.sh scripts with the chosen configurations.",
      "most_likely_effort": 2
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A10",
      "activity_name": "Kafka benchmark execution and analysis (U5)",
      "activity_description": "Execute producer and consumer benchmark runs, collect throughput and latency metrics, compare against the LinkedIn Kafka baseline, and produce an analysis summary.",
      "most_likely_effort": 3
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A11",
      "activity_name": "Rabbit benchmarking environment setup (U1)",
      "activity_description": "Provision the RabbitMQ cluster on EC2 (per the same class of infrastructure as Kafka), install the rabbitmq-perf-test tool, and prepare workload scripts.",
      "most_likely_effort": 2
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A12",
      "activity_name": "Rabbit benchmark execution and analysis (U1)",
      "activity_description": "Run rabbitmq-perf-test producer/consumer workloads, collect results, and produce an analysis suitable for use as XD cluster sizing reference.",
      "most_likely_effort": 3
    },
    {
      "activity_id": "A13",
      "activity_name": "Final results documentation and reporting",
      "activity_description": "Consolidate the U1 and U5 benchmark results, link the documentation, and produce a summary document that developers can use as reference for XD cluster setup.",
      "most_likely_effort": 2
    }
  ],
  "reported_total": {
    "most_likely_effort": 28
  }
}
```

